# 10/12-bit inverse transforms in 32-bit NEON lanes: the t=1 measurement

date: 2026-08-07
host: Apple M4 Pro (8 P-core + 4 E-core = 12 logical), macOS 26.5.2, aarch64
build: stable rustc, `--release` (lto="fat", codegen-units=1), DEFAULT features
       (bitdepth_8 + bitdepth_16, CHECKED DisjointMut, `forbid(unsafe_code)`).
       No `-C target-cpu=native`. No `nice` on any timed run.
base:  fix/aarch64-mc-itx @ 2c7c082 (PR #453)
arm:   perf/lr-itx-neon @ 560f4ec + 67accdd (itx 16bpc + the LR deletion)
ref:   dav1d 1.5.4 (homebrew), `--framedelay 1` (tile threading only, the model
       we implement)
vectors: 3840x2160, 4 tile cols x 2 tile rows.
       rav1d-safe decodes `v4k_8tile{,_10b}.avif`; dav1d decodes the IVF twin
       verified to be the same key frame repeated.

## Instrument

`scripts/perf/verify_gap.sh` unchanged: one process per (arm, cell), each run at
2 and at 20 frames, `beta = (ms20 - ms2) / 18` so process startup drops out;
arm order rotated per round; a round is DISCARDED and re-run whole if any
foreign process exceeds 25% CPU during it. Every cell below committed with
**foreign = 0**. Reported figure is the median beta over n rounds.
Raw: `benchmarks/itx_hbd_gap_2026-08-07.tsv`
(round, arm, vector, threads, 2, ms2, 20, ms20, foreign).

## Result

ms/frame, and the ratio to dav1d --framedelay 1:

    vector           t   base     arm    dav1d   base/dav1d  arm/dav1d   arm/base
    v4k_8tile        1   398.5   397.7   246.6      1.62       1.61       0.998
    v4k_8tile        2   223.6   222.9   125.2      1.79       1.78       0.997
    v4k_8tile        4   132.1   130.9    65.7      2.01       1.99       0.991
    v4k_8tile        8   116.6   118.2    37.1      3.15       3.19       1.014
    v4k_8tile_10b    1   508.3   455.8   251.4      2.02       1.81       0.897
    v4k_8tile_10b    2   276.1   260.6   127.7      2.16       2.04       0.944
    v4k_8tile_10b    4   162.9   156.0    66.9      2.43       2.33       0.958
    v4k_8tile_10b    8   141.3   142.8    38.7      3.65       3.69       1.011
                                              n = 7 at t=1, 5 at t=2/4/8

**10bpc t=1: 508.3 -> 455.8 ms/frame, 1.115x, ratio 2.02 -> 1.81.** t=2 and t=4
carry 5.6% and 4.2%.

The 8bpc column is the control and is flat, as it must be: the change adds one
`BPC16` arm to `itxfm_add_dispatch` and touches no 8bpc kernel. Read the 8bpc
row as "no regression", not as a result.

**The t=8 cells are +1.4% (8bpc) and +1.1% (arm slower).** Both bit depths move
the same way, including the 8bpc control that contains none of the new code, so
this is the t=8 cell's own dispatch/tracker noise and not an effect of the
change — but it is a real measured number and it is reported rather than
dropped. t=8 is also the inverted regime on this machine (t=4 -> t=8 is 0.88-0.92x
for us against 0.56-0.58x for dav1d); it is tracker-bound, not kernel-bound,
which is why a kernel win does not show there.

## Where the time went

`/usr/bin/sample`, t=1, self+inclusive via `scripts/perf/sample_selftime.py` and
`sample_tree.py`, `v4k_8tile_10b`:

    itx inclusive                     21.93%  ->  12.79%
    itx_1d::inv_dct4_1d_internal_c     6.37%  ->  absent
    itx_1d::inv_dct8_1d_internal_c     3.88%  ->  absent
    itx_1d::inv_dct16_1d_internal_c    2.56%  ->  absent
    itx_1d::inv_adst8_1d_internal_c    1.26%  ->  absent
    itx::inv_txfm_add::<BitDepth16>    4.94%  ->  absent  (self, the 2-D glue)
    itx_arm_hbd::apply1d                  --  ->   2.43%  (self)
    itxfm::Fn::call::<BitDepth16>         --  ->   4.10%  (self, inlined driver)

No `itx_1d` sample remains: every inverse transform this bitstream asks for now
runs in NEON. **Of the 12.79% that is left, 5.88 points are `block_mut` guard
traffic** — `mut_slice_as` 3.29 + `remove_wide` 2.59 — and only ~6.5 points are
transform work. That is the same `block_mut` cost the P2 profile named; it is
now the dominant term in itx and is owned by whoever is working
`include/dav1d/picture.rs`. This branch deliberately did not touch that file.

Also confirms a P2 prediction: `inv_dct32_1d` and `inv_dct64_1d` take **zero**
samples on this vector, which is why `hbd_supported()` stopping at
`max(w, h) <= 16` costs nothing here. On content with 32/64-point transforms it
will; those shapes still run the scalar reference.

## Correctness

* 766/766 dav1d-test-data (2 SKIP are non-IVF), and the set-diff is BY NAME
  against `benchmarks/aarch64_md5_fixes_2026-08-07_final.tsv.zst`: every vector
  has the same status AND the same *actual* MD5. A change that repaired some
  and broke others would be invisible in a count and is not invisible here.
* `examples/bench_ab_decode`'s CHECKSUM line agrees between arms on both perf
  vectors.
* `safe_simd/itx_arm_parity.rs` gains four 16bpc arms driving the real
  dispatcher against `itx::itxfm_add_scalar_fallback`: 10bpc and 12bpc, small
  and large coefficients, and every legal `eob` at 10bpc (12,544 cells). The
  `Report::finish` liveness gate asserts every cell took the NEON path, so a
  shape the dispatch refuses cannot pass as scalar-vs-scalar.

### Mutation proofs (the sweep detects what it claims to)

Each planted, test run, reverted, green re-confirmed:

  1. `dct4`'s 1567 -> 1568 — 877 of 2,848 cells diverge, all four arms fail.
  2. `transpose4x4` returns rows 0,2,1,3 — 12,085 of 12,544 cells diverge.
  3. Row/column clips replaced with `i16::MIN..=i16::MAX`, i.e. **exactly the
     defect the existing `itx_arm_neon_*` 16bpc entry points carry** — the two
     *small*-coefficient arms still PASS; only the large-coefficient arms fail
     (633/2,848 at 10bpc, 2,438/2,848 at 12bpc).

Mutation 3 is the reason the large-coefficient arms exist. A port validated
only on light residual would look bit-exact and ship wrong pixels on real
10-bit content.

## Follow-up the same day: the wide guard was eating the win

The 12.79% that remained above was 5.88 points of `block_mut` guard traffic.
The CfL kernel landed in between and made the mechanism unmissable, so both
were changed to take **one narrow guard per row** instead of one wide guard per
block — the extent the scalar reference already uses, and the *narrower* of the
two, so nothing about tile-thread safety loosens.

The CfL measurement is what established it (same driver as the LR A/B:
`scripts/perf/bin_ab.sh`, one process per cell, arm order rotated per round,
median of 9, raw in `benchmarks/narrow_guard_ab_2026-08-07.tsv`):

    v4k_8tile 8bpc, t=1        ms/frame   ratio to no-CfL-NEON   foreign
      no CfL NEON (hbd)         398.19      1.0000               0
      CfL NEON, wide guard      397.63      0.9986               0
      CfL NEON, per-row guard   394.23      0.9901               0

A 2.46%-of-self-time kernel bought 0.14% of wall behind a wide guard, because
the wide guard cost +0.79% `add_wide::<true>` and +0.73% `remove_wide` (profile
diff of the two 8bpc `sample` runs). Per-row guards turn the same kernel into
0.99%.

Applying the same shape to the 16bpc itx path:

    v4k_8tile_10b, t=1         ms/frame   ratio to base          foreign
      base (2c7c082)            519.93      1.0000               0..3
      itx NEON, block_mut       460.57      0.8858
      itx NEON, per-row guard   443.76      0.8535

i.e. another ~3.2% on top of the transform port. **Caveat: a sibling agent's
benchmark was resident for part of that 10bpc run (`foreign` up to 3), so
those three are an interleaved same-vector ratio and not idle-box absolutes.**
The idle-box absolutes on record are the base-vs-`block_mut` table above
(508.3 -> 455.8 at 10bpc t=1, foreign 0 throughout); the per-row increment did
not get a clean window before the session ended and is reported as a ratio
only.

## What this does NOT say

* Nothing was measured on inter-heavy or 32/64-transform content. One 4K intra
  still per bit depth.
* No claim about `unchecked` builds; every number is the checked default.
* The 32- and 64-point 16bpc transforms are NOT ported. They are named in
  `hbd_supported()` and they run the scalar reference.
* `Kind::Identity` at n=32 and the WHT 4x4 lossless transform are likewise not
  ported.
